From: smadden @ csu.EDU.AU ("Steve Madden (Unix Systems Programmer)") =20

Hi Craig,

Yes I have an operator menu that activates when operator logs in.
One of the options is change password, upon selecting that they are=20
prompted for a username, then for a change password password (so they can=
=20
leave the menu up all day and not worry about abuse at lunch or whatever)=
=20
so an admin passwd must be supplied for each change. Then I check the=20
group of the user they are trying to change, they are only allowed to=20
change gid 7000 and the whole occurance is logged in /var/adm. It is=20
secure, captures all sigs, and logs them out if they try anything tricky.

Sounds good doesn't it?  Well this is how I did it...

 1) I wrote the menu program in Perl, which resides in their home directo=
ry.
 2) The menu option that calls for a password change, gets the user=20
    information, checks the validity (they are allowed), and calls a=20
    passwd.pl program - also written in perl.
 3) The passwd.pl program first takes in the username parameter and then=20
    asks for the supervisor password.
 4) If the given password is correct and a valid userid is passed, the sc=
ript
    (This part is cute) writes its own little (5 line) c program to force=
 the
    execution of passwd as root for that user only.
 5) Then it compiles it, runs it and removes it.
 6) The user only sees enter username, and if valid enter new password an=
d
    verify new password and then returns to the menu.=20
 7) It sounds slow, but its actually so fast no-one believed thats how I =
was
    doing it.

Scripts and Code to those genuinely impressed and willing to leave my=20
name intact in the scripts.

Steve..



