Well, I received responses from the following:

	Tom Leffingwell	sysman@homer.bus.miami.edu
	Ryan R. Ziegler	zieglerr@novachem.com
	Martin Moore	martin@decatl.alf.dec.com
	Brian Sheehan	sheehan@scripps.edu
	Gustavo		dasilva@bbs.elogica.com.br
 
The concensus was...just force the account to have the root UID by editting
both the password file and the /tcp/files/auth/s/shutdown file.

This did get me closer, but what finally crested the wave was ...
I placed an echo blah blah  statement immediately prior to the /sbin/init s
statement.

I have since changed the /sbin/init statement to a /sbin/shutdown statement, but 
all is well now.

Martin Moore did suggest that I create a C program to execute the init statement
and then set the permissions bit to run this program as root.  I won't let that
idea go to waste!


