>to them, choose Application Manager --> System Management Utilities.
>Well, I don't have a fancy console display on my machines.  What I have are 
>PC's running x-emulators (eXceed to be exact).  Previously to run XIsso or
>XSysAdmin, I just set my display (setenv DISPLAY) to be my pc and ran the 
>command (/usr/tcb/bin/XIsso for example).  Everything worked ok.  
>
>How do I get the new stuff started?  I've found some stuff in /usr/var/.dt/
>appconfig/appmanager/C/System_Admin/... like DailyAdmin/Dxaccounts but when
>I try to run anything, I get "Common Desktop Environment DT action" and nothing
>happens.
>
>I found some other stuff in /usr/dt/bin like Xsession.  The Xsession looks 
>like it's starting up the Common Desktop Environment but all I get is a 
>HelpViewer popup.  I even figured well maybe since I started the Desktop
>I can then run those other apps, but I still get the "Common Desktop DT 
>Environment DT action" messages.  I can run things like dtcalc under the
>/usr/dt/bin directory.
>
>Can anyone help?

Answer from Randy Hayman:

To find what any of the CDE actions actually run look for the *.dt file
of the same name, or of the resource name.

For your particular need (providing $LANG=C)

haymanr@icefog> grep -i account /usr/dt/appconfig/types/C/*.dt            
dailyadmin_sm.dt:ACTION Dxaccounts
dailyadmin_sm.dt:       LABEL   Account Manager
dailyadmin_sm.dt:       EXEC_STRING     /usr/sbin/dop -n /usr/bin/X11/dxaccounts
dailyadmin_sm.dt:       DESCRIPTION     This action invokes the account manager 
application


meaning that /usr/bin/X11/dxaccounts is the executable you need.


Randy M. Hayman
haymanr@icefog.alaska.edu

